1
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:04,957
Every AI agent has a contract it's
supposed to follow. Mine has four steps:

2
00:00:04,997 --> 00:00:09,572
plan, call a tool, validate
the result, then respond.

3
00:00:10,012 --> 00:00:14,432
We trust agents to follow that order,
and then we basically never check.

4
00:00:14,832 --> 00:00:19,212
A trace shows you one run. An
eval scores the final answer.

5
00:00:19,252 --> 00:00:23,122
Neither one tells you how often the
agent actually finished thinking.

6
00:00:23,388 --> 00:00:27,895
So I measured it two ways, on the
same hundred and twenty-five traces.

7
00:00:28,405 --> 00:00:33,005
A normal span counter said my validate
step ran in one hundred percent of runs.

8
00:00:33,530 --> 00:00:35,965
A funnel said sixty-four percent.

9
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:42,680
Same traces. Both queries correct. Only
one of them was asking the right question.

10
00:00:43,047 --> 00:00:47,053
This is a funnel over the agent's
reasoning, running against SigNoz.

11
00:00:47,613 --> 00:00:50,248
A hundred and twenty-five runs start.

12
00:00:50,313 --> 00:00:53,253
A hundred and twenty-five
reach the tool call.

13
00:00:53,698 --> 00:00:58,118
Then it falls off a cliff — only
eighty reach validate, in order.

14
00:00:58,158 --> 00:00:59,988
Sixty-four percent.

15
00:01:00,438 --> 00:01:03,123
Notice the counts are
printed on every bar.

16
00:01:03,183 --> 00:01:06,218
A percentage without a
denominator isn't evidence.

17
00:01:06,549 --> 00:01:09,424
And this isn't my tool
marking its own homework.

18
00:01:09,464 --> 00:01:12,374
Here's the same funnel
inside SigNoz itself.

19
00:01:12,854 --> 00:01:19,179
Conversion rate: sixty-four percent.
Total spans: one two five, one two five,

20
00:01:19,219 --> 00:01:23,634
eighty, eighty. Down thirty-six
percent on step three.

21
00:01:23,994 --> 00:01:27,079
Trace Funnels are a SigNoz
primitive — Langfuse,

22
00:01:27,119 --> 00:01:30,329
LangSmith and Phoenix don't
expose anything like it.

23
00:01:30,704 --> 00:01:33,734
So why did the counter
say one hundred percent?

24
00:01:34,184 --> 00:01:38,576
Because a counter only asks: did this
span exist anywhere in the trace.

25
00:01:38,726 --> 00:01:40,676
It has no idea where.

26
00:01:41,006 --> 00:01:44,566
A funnel asks: did it happen
after the previous step.

27
00:01:45,026 --> 00:01:49,756
Forty-five runs emitted a validate
span — before the tool result existed.

28
00:01:49,816 --> 00:01:52,946
The agent validated an answer
that hadn't arrived yet.

29
00:01:53,376 --> 00:01:57,681
Every one of those is a success to a
counter, and a failure to a funnel.

30
00:01:57,721 --> 00:02:01,096
Ordering is a property of
the trace, not of the span.

31
00:02:01,615 --> 00:02:07,750
Here's one of them. Plan, then validate
— in red — and only then the tool call.

32
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:12,790
Validate finished before the tool it
was checking even started. Nine spans,

33
00:02:12,830 --> 00:02:16,450
one error, and the run still
returned a confident answer.

34
00:02:16,797 --> 00:02:22,232
Zero of SigNoz's forty-one MCP tools touch
funnels, so I shipped the missing ones.

35
00:02:22,749 --> 00:02:26,444
Now the agent calls get funnel analytics
and reads its own conversion rate —

36
00:02:26,484 --> 00:02:29,014
a loop that wasn't expressible before.

37
00:02:29,429 --> 00:02:34,884
And the read path has no model in it.
It's sub-second, and it costs nothing.

38
00:02:35,190 --> 00:02:36,805
Which means it can just run:

39
00:02:36,845 --> 00:02:41,370
a threshold rule that goes red when the
agent stops validating before it answers.

40
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:47,337
Watch validate-step conversion. Alert
on the cliff. That's the whole idea.
